<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boxing Day</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Offering Day</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boxing Day is a holiday celebrated on 26 December, the day after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day, which originated in the United Kingdom and is kept in several Commonwealth nations. It was once a day for giving gifts to those in need and usually coincides with the Christian festival of Saint Stephen's Day. The origin of the name is not definitive: it may refer to the alms boxes placed in churches, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> tradesmen collected &quot;Christmas boxes&quot; of money or presents after Christmas, a custom mentioned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-pepys">Samuel Pepys</a>'s diary in 1663. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> English Dictionary gives the earliest attestation from Britain in 1743. In the United Kingdom outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, 26 December has been a bank holiday since 1871.</p></div>
